Log in to an observe server with a dashboard username and password.

  The server checks the password and returns a user API key (nxu_...), saved to
  ~/.nexora/credentials, one per server, readable only by you. `nexora cancel`
  and `nexora replay` then send it automatically. The key lasts 12 hours, and
  ends on `nexora logout`, a password change, user removal or a server restart.

  Key order for cancel and replay: --api-key, then NEXORA_API_KEY, then the key
  saved by this command.

  Five wrong passwords lock the username for 15 minutes.


  Over plain http:// to another machine the key or password could be read on
  the way, so the command refuses unless you pass --allow-insecure-http. Use an
  https:// URL (for example behind a TLS proxy) instead. Localhost is fine.
  Exit codes
  ──────────
  0  Logged in and the key was saved
  1  Wrong username or password, throttled, or the server could not be reached
  2  Invalid arguments

  Examples
  ────────
  Log in to the local observe server (prompts for username and password):
    nexora login

  Log in to a remote server non-interactively:
    printf '%s\n' "$PASSWORD" | nexora login --server https://nexora.internal:9464 -u alice --password-stdin
